al research so  um and  and i think of it at the end um you know  the quality of a product really is  um proportional i guess is dependent on  the actual effort put into the  production of that product right and so  if you have a so-so standard then  probably at the end it's going to  reflect in your research data it's going  to be uh so-so all right  okay  so  moving on to animal production  um i  like to simplify that and for the lay  people out there you know the general  flow of things production is such okay  you have a breeding colony you know  animals have to come from somewhere so  breeding population  and this population has to have  a regular monitoring of their health  profile their pedigree information is  probably tracked  their production performance should be  tracked and there are some  measures in place to prevent  cross-contamination whether it be  against microbial cause contamination or  from genetic cross-contamination  okay  and all those animals once they're born  they go into a stock population and  these are the population that then gets  sold to the customers okay  and then in this population you want to  pay attention to the rearing environment  you know the staff that are working with  them  um you want to pay attention to the  model phenotype  and also the availability of these  models  okay and before the animals go out the  door there is generally a quality  control component associated okay  there's genetic monitoring hopefully  there's health monitoring  if there are sick animals or abnormal  animals veterinary care is provided  and all of this operation of course  is carried out following sets of  standard operating procedures okay  and then as they go out the door there's  you know you want to consider the  shipping  to transportation so the containers  itself whether or not is suitable for  the species  the transportation vehicle is it climate  controlled um is it  properly cleaned out before and after  shipments you know some of 